<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  WDS CLIENT UNATTEND - ARM64
  ============================================================================

  WHAT THIS FILE IS
    A WDS *client* unattend file. It carries the windowsPE pass only - the
    phase that runs inside the WinPE boot image after the machine PXE-boots,
    before any image is applied. Its job is to automate the WDS client
    screens.

  IT IS NOT PER-IMAGE
    WDS allows exactly ONE client unattend per architecture per server. This
    file is therefore shared by every ARM64 image you deploy -
    Windows 7, 8.1 and 10 alike. That is why it contains nothing
    version-specific. Everything version-specific lives in the per-image
    files (image-unattend-*.xml).

  HOW TO ASSIGN IT (Server 2019 WDS console)
    Copy this file to  C:\RemoteInstall\WdsClientUnattend\  on the server,
    then: right-click the server -> Properties -> Client tab
    -> tick "Enable unattended installation"
    -> next to ARM64 click Browse... and pick this file -> OK.

  BEFORE THIS FILE CAN DO ANYTHING: THE BOOT IMAGE
    A client unattend only runs inside a WinPE boot image of the SAME
    architecture. WDS cannot serve an ARM64 client from an x64 boot image, so
    this file does nothing until an ARM64 boot image exists on the server.

    That is the hard part, not this file. ARM64 PXE deployment is genuinely
    awkward:
      - The ARM64 boot.wim has to come from a recent Windows ADK, and you have
        to inject the machine's NIC and storage drivers into it. x86 and x64
        drivers will not work - they have to be ARM64 drivers, and many vendors
        still do not publish them.
      - Without a working NIC driver WinPE starts and then immediately reboots,
        which looks like a PXE failure but is not.
      - Many ARM64 laptops have no Ethernet port and no usable network-boot
        entry in firmware at all. Snapdragon machines in particular are widely
        reported to PXE-boot and then drop straight back to the UEFI menu, even
        through a USB or Thunderbolt Ethernet dongle.
      - Check whether your Server 2019 Client tab even lists an ARM64 slot. If
        it does not, this file cannot be assigned server-wide; you would have to
        attach it to a prestaged computer object instead.

    None of that is a reason not to have the file. It is a reason to expect the
    USB route to be the one that actually works on a Lenovo Yoga.

  WHAT IT DOES
    Sets the WinPE locale so the client skips the "choose your locale and
    keyboard" screen that WDS shows before the logon prompt. That is all.

  WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
    - No <Credentials>. You will be prompted for domain credentials at the
      client, which is the safer behaviour. A credentials block here is
      stored in PLAIN TEXT on the server and sent to the client over the
      network. If you accept that and want it anyway, uncomment the block
      below and fill it in - but read wds/README.md first, because there is
      a live CVE about exactly this.
    - No <ImageSelection>. You pick the image at the client. If you set it,
      WDS also requires <InstallTo>, which in turn requires a
      <DiskConfiguration> that partitions the disk automatically - and that
      wipes the target without asking. Left out on purpose, same as the USB
      answer files.
    - No <DiskConfiguration>. "Where do you want to install Windows?" prompts
      normally. Nothing in this file can wipe a disk unattended.
-->
<unattend xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:unattend">

    <settings pass="windowsPE">

        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-International-Core-WinPE" processorArchitecture="arm64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <SetupUILanguage>
                <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            </SetupUILanguage>
            <InputLocale>en-US</InputLocale>
            <SystemLocale>en-US</SystemLocale>
            <UILanguage>en-US</UILanguage>
            <UILanguageFallback>en-US</UILanguageFallback>
            <UserLocale>en-US</UserLocale>
        </component>

        <!-- Uncomment to log the WDS client in automatically. Plain text on
             disk and on the wire - see wds/README.md.
        <component name="Microsoft-Windows-Setup" processorArchitecture="arm64" publicKeyToken="31bf3856ad364e35" language="neutral" versionScope="nonSxS" xmlns:wcm="http://schemas.microsoft.com/WMIConfig/2002/State" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
            <WindowsDeploymentServices>
                <Login>
                    <Credentials>
                        <Domain>YOURDOMAIN</Domain>
                        <Username>wdsuser</Username>
                        <Password>changeme</Password>
                    </Credentials>
                </Login>
            </WindowsDeploymentServices>
        </component>
        -->

    </settings>

</unattend>
